## How SEPA Bank Transfers Work (For EU Senders)

### What is SEPA?
SEPA aims to make cross-border Euro transfers within this area equivalent to a domestic transfer within your own country. SEPA is made up of the Eurozone, countries within the EU and a few other countries which also support Euro bank transfers.

In plain terms: sending EUR from a German bank to a Belgian IBAN costs the same as sending EUR from your German bank to another German bank — which is almost always **free**.

---

### The Setup (Step by Step)

**Your friend in NZ does this once:**
1. Opens a **Wise account** (he's the only one who needs one).
2. Opens a **EUR balance** inside Wise.
3. Once he has his EUR account details, he'll have his own account details where he can receive euros. This isn't a bank account, but he can receive money in the same way by sharing his IBAN with friends, family, clients or companies, so that they can send him money.
4. Wise has an entity in Belgium, so they use a Belgian BIC code and a Wise address in Belgium for some of their European currency accounts. So his IBAN will look like a Belgian one (starts with **BE**).
5. He shares this IBAN + his name with all of you.

**Each EU sender does this every month:**
1. Opens their normal banking app (Sparkasse, DKB, ING, N26, whatever).
2. Adds the friend's Wise **IBAN** (the Belgian one) as a recipient.
3. Sends a normal **SEPA transfer** of €10 (or whatever amount).
4. Done. No app to download. No ID to upload. No account to create.

---

### What It Costs

**On your (sender) side:**
- If you're sending money from a bank based in the SEPA zone (e.g. UK, France, Switzerland), sending money to one of Wise's Euro accounts shouldn't cost any more than a regular, local transfer. In the majority of cases, this means it's free.
- Your German bank sees a Belgian IBAN and treats it as a normal eurozone transfer. **€0 fee.**

**On your friend's (receiver) side:**
- It's free to send and receive money from one Wise currency to another, as long as they're the same currency.
- When receiving money to your Wise account, it's free to receive domestic payments in all currencies. SEPA counts as "domestic" for EUR.
- Add money to Wise Account via bank transfer for free when the currency stays the same.

**So the flow looks like:**

| Step | Who | Action | Fee |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | You (Germany) | SEPA transfer €10 → friend's Wise BE IBAN | **€0** |
| 2 | Friend (NZ) | Receives €10 in Wise EUR balance | **€0** |
| 3 | Friend (NZ) | Converts EUR → NZD inside Wise | **~0.5%** (~€0.05) |
| 4 | Friend (NZ) | Spends via Wise card or withdraws to NZ bank | **Free** (card) or small fixed fee (bank) |

---

### Why This Is the Best Option for Your Group

- **EU senders need ZERO apps, ZERO accounts, ZERO ID uploads.** They just do a bank transfer like paying rent.
- **Only the friend in NZ needs a Wise account.** He's the one who uploaded his ID once.
- Standard SEPA transfers are completed within one to two working days. SEPA Instant Credit Transfers can be completed within five seconds.
- The **only** fee in the entire chain is the ~0.5% conversion (EUR→NZD) that your friend pays when he decides to convert. On €10 that's about **5 cents**.

---

### What About Non-EU Senders?

This is where it gets slightly more complicated. SEPA only works for people with bank accounts in the SEPA zone. For senders outside the EU:

- They could send via **SWIFT** to the same Wise EUR IBAN, but there is a fee to receive international (Swift) payments in most currencies. Receiving EUR Swift payments has a fixed fee per payment of 2.39 EUR.
- **Better option:** Non-EU senders open their own Wise account and send Wise-to-Wise (same currency, free). But then they need to upload ID too.

